  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- P(disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(no disease) = 0.998\n- P(test+ | disease) = 0.98 (sensitivity)\n- P(test+ | no disease) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(test+) = (0.002 × 0.98) + (0.998 × 0.05) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(disease | test+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**As a percentage:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a consequence of the low base rate (1/500) combined with a nontrivial false-positive rate (5%) that swamps the true positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
